From 27a8b2306e3cb5791c97554a9b69b8e2e83f0432 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tessera CI <ci@tessera.test>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 13:56:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] fix(risk): restore the fraud-model cache TTL and bound the
 call policy

INC-4412. CACHE_TTL_SECONDS was set to 0 in b745a5a, shipped in the 14:02
rollout recorded against 4b56e1d. The response cache was absorbing ~77% of
scoring calls, so disabling it put ~420 rps on a dependency with ~155 rps of
capacity. Retries without backoff amplified that to ~1120 rps and the system
settled into a metastable bad state: 70.8% authorization success, p99 pinned
at the 8.5s edge budget, no self-recovery.

Root cause:
  - CACHE_TTL_SECONDS 0 -> 300.

Contributing factors closed here:
  - REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS None -> 1.0. An unbounded attempt consumes the
    whole edge budget, which is why p99 sat at 8.5s.
  - RETRY_ATTEMPTS 3 -> 2, backoff 0.0 -> 0.05, jitter off -> on, and jitter
    is now actually applied in client.py. It was previously read only by the
    capacity model and the ops console.
  - MAX_CONNECTIONS 512 -> 256, reverting 087e524, which shipped in the same
    rollout and removed the only backpressure the gateway had.
  - _ResponseCache now prunes expired entries and is bounded by entry count.
    It had no eviction path at all, which is the unbounded growth TESS-2287
    was chasing -- setting the TTL to 0 did not reclaim anything.

Left deliberately alone: CIRCUIT_BREAKER_ENABLED stays False and is now
documented as unimplemented in client.py, so nobody mitigates by flipping a
flag that only changes the dashboard.

The capacity model against the fixed config returns the observed pre-incident
steady state on every axis: 96.2 rps, 77% hit rate, p99 0.54s, 100% success.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
